# **Driving the JIB VI.** The demo hub, with the boat out in open water and the
# player already standing on the helm mat.
#
# `extends`, not a copy: the rig, the rails, the supplies, the boat and her
# decks all come from `assets/games/deeper_demo.loom`. **Two fields differ** —
# where the boat is and where the player is — and the movement script is the
# shipped `deeper_player.rhai`, unmodified. That last part is the entire point
# of this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS SCENE HAS NO PILOT SCRIPT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every other input-driven test in this repository swaps the movement model for
# a pilot that fabricates the press — the five fight benches, `rig_walk.loom`.
# That is the right shape when the thing under test is *downstream* of the
# press (the fight, the rails), and the wrong shape when the thing under test
# **is the mapping from the press**. The helm reads `move_x` and `move_z`
# straight off the input, so a pilot writing `helm_force` itself would be a
# test of the pilot: exactly the "test that cannot fail" this branch has
# already shipped once.
#
# So `loom sim --hold` exists instead. It writes `Runner::input`, the same
# field `loom run` writes when a human holds W. Two runs with different holds
# answer every question here, which is why it is a constant and not a schedule.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY SHE IS OUT IN OPEN WATER AND NOT IN HER BERTH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Because a berth is a wall on one side. Berthed at z = -10.45 her starboard
# bulwark is 0.15 m off the quay, so a turn *to starboard* swings her bow into
# it and she stalls — the first version of this file asserted a starboard turn
# from the berth and failed, correctly, on a boat behaving like a boat. Leaving
# the berth is its own manoeuvre and is tested where it belongs, on the demo
# scene itself: walk aboard holding W and she is 34 m clear inside fifteen
# seconds.
#
# Thirty metres further out (z = -40) is far enough that nothing static is
# inside a full-lock turning circle, which is about 28 m of radius. The player
# sits at boat-local (-7.00, -, -1.80), the port side of the aft cockpit and
# the middle of `HelmMat`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT MEASURES — five runs, because they are five claims
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   # 1. hands off the keys: she stays exactly where she is
#   loom sim assets/test/rig_drive.loom --ticks 900 \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.x < 3.1" --assert "Rig/Boat.x > 2.9" \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.z < -39.9" --assert "Rig/Boat.z > -40.1" \
#     --assert "Rig/Player.y > 1.2"
#
#   # 2. W is ahead, and she stays a boat while she does it
#   loom sim assets/test/rig_drive.loom --ticks 900 --hold move_z=1 \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.x > 40.0" --assert "Rig/Boat.y > -0.4" \
#     --assert "Rig/Player.y > 1.2"
#
#   # 3. S is astern
#   loom sim assets/test/rig_drive.loom --ticks 900 --hold move_z=-1 \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.x < -20.0" --assert "Rig/Boat.y > -0.4" \
#     --assert "Rig/Player.y > 1.2"
#
#   # 4. D puts the wheel to starboard, which on this hull is +Z
#   loom sim assets/test/rig_drive.loom --ticks 900 --hold "move_z=1,move_x=1" \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.z > -20.0" --assert "Rig/Boat.x > 20.0" \
#     --assert "Rig/Player.y > 1.2"
#
#   # 5. and A is the mirror of it
#   loom sim assets/test/rig_drive.loom --ticks 900 --hold "move_z=1,move_x=-1" \
#     --assert "Rig/Boat.z < -60.0" --assert "Rig/Boat.x > 20.0" \
#     --assert "Rig/Player.y > 1.2"
#
# **Runs 4 and 5 are a pair on purpose.** A single turn assertion passes on a
# hull that always swings the same way — a sign error in the torque, a list, a
# wake pushing her over — and the mirror is what makes it a claim about the
# wheel. Measured, at 900 ticks: +26.5 x and +28.4 z one way, +26.4 x and
# -28.5 z the other. Symmetric to within four centimetres over thirty metres.
#
# **`Rig/Player.y > 1.2` on every run is the rider still being aboard**, and it
# is on every run because it is the claim that failed first. The deck stands a
# capsule's centre at about 1.60 and the sea holds a swimmer at -0.70, so the
# band cannot be satisfied by the wrong one. See the long note in
# `deeper_player.rhai` beside `if grounded { vy = velocity[1]; }` for what was
# happening and why the fix is in a script rather than in `loom_physics`.
#
# **`Rig/Boat.y > -0.4` is the seven-knot wall, watched rather than assumed.**
# ADR 0063 swept this hull: 1.6 MN settles at 3.586 m/s and holds her trim,
# 2.0 MN walks it, and 2.63 MN submarines her to y = -14.34 because thrust is
# body-frame and a bow that digs in turns the vector downward. The helm's
# 1.1 MN measures 2.96 m/s — 5.8 knots — with her trim inside 4 cm, and this
# assertion is what says so on every run rather than once in a header.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "0c6e4b19-52a7-4d38-b1f6-9e73a5c8d240"

[[prefab]]
key = "demo"
id = "b2a43f8f-314a-4d92-b4aa-322411869fcd"
path = "../games/deeper_demo.loom"

[[node]]
name = "Rig"
extends = "demo"

[[node]]
name = "Boat"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [3.0, 0.0, -40.0] }

[[node]]
name = "Player"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [-4.00, 1.62, -41.80] }

# **The channel marks, shoved forty metres clear.** The hub lays a line of
# buoys nine and a half metres off the boat's track so the helmsman can see he
# is moving; they are static colliders, and this scene exists to turn her hard
# both ways in open water. Measured with them where the hub puts them: the
# starboard-turn run walked into one at z = -27.3 and put the rider in the sea.
# A boat that stops on a navigation mark is correct; a turning-circle bench
# that measures one is not.
[[node]]
name = "Marks"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 0.0, 60.0] }
